---
const users = [
  {
    tag: "User A",
    who: "The homeowner",
    color: "violet",
    need: "Researching contractors before they spend five figures on a patio. They need proof the work is real and a reason to trust him before they ever pick up the phone.",
  },
  {
    tag: "User B",
    who: "The owner",
    color: "amber",
    need: "Bilingual, Spanish-speaking, runs everything from his phone, not tech-savvy, already fluent in Instagram. He needs to add new work himself, the day he finishes a job, without calling me.",
  },
];
---

<!-- Two Users, Two Needs -->
<section class="mb-24">
  <div class="mb-8">
    <span
      class="text-xs font-semibold text-amber-700 tracking-widest uppercase"
    >
      02 — Two Users, Two Needs
    </span>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-2">
      One site. Two people who wanted opposite things.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4 text-lg max-w-3xl">
      A portfolio site usually has one audience. This one had two, and serving
      one well meant nothing if it broke the other.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
    {
      users.map((u) => (
        <div
          class:list={[
            "rounded-2xl border bg-white p-6 shadow-sm",
            u.color === "violet" ? "border-violet-200" : "border-amber-200",
          ]}
        >
          <div
            class:list={[
              "inline-block px-2.5 py-1 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest rounded-full border mb-3",
              u.color === "violet"
                ? "bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 border-violet-200"
                : "bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 border-amber-200",
            ]}
          >
            {u.tag}
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-semibold text-xl mb-2">{u.who}</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">{u.need}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-6 text-lg max-w-3xl">
    Every decision on this page answers to one of them. If a choice didn't earn
    the homeowner's trust or protect the owner's independence, it didn't make
    the cut.
  </p>
</section>
